Therefore, the entire product is: $$
\prod_{k=2}^{n} \frac{\ln\left(\frac{1}{n - k + 2}\right)}{\ln k} = \prod_{k=2}^{n} \frac{-\ln(n - k + 2)}{\ln k}
$$

Therefore: $$
(-1)^{n - 1} \prod_{k=2}^{n} \frac{\ln(n - k + 2)}{\ln k}
$$

So the original product becomes: $$
(-1)^{n - 1} \cdot 1 = (-1)^{n - 1}
$$
